format: equavela-worksheet/3
page:
  size: A4
  margins: {top: 54, right: 54, bottom: 54, left: 54}
settings:
  format.precision: 4
regions:
  - id: title
    page: 1
    at: [54, 54]
    text:
      width: full
      text: |
        {style=title}Frequency response of an RC low-pass filter

        A resistor and a capacitor form the simplest low-pass filter. Its transfer function is a complex function of frequency; magnitude and phase are drawn on logarithmic axes, the way a Bode plot is read.
  - id: h1
    page: 1
    at: [54, 174]
    text: "{style=heading1}Components and cut-off"
  - id: t1
    page: 1
    at: [54, 200]
    text:
      width: full
      text: Units travel through the calculation, so the cut-off frequency comes out in hertz and is shown in kilohertz.
  - id: R
    page: 1
    at: [54, 228]
    math: "R := 1 kΩ@unit"
  - id: C
    page: 1
    at: [170, 228]
    math: "C := 100 nF@unit"
  - id: tau
    page: 1
    at: [300, 228]
    math: "τ := R·C"
  - id: tauv
    page: 1
    at: [400, 228]
    math: "τ = … μs"
  - id: fc
    page: 1
    at: [54, 262]
    math: "f_c := 1/(2·π·τ)"
  - id: fcv
    page: 1
    at: [210, 262]
    math: "f_c = … kHz"
  - id: h2
    page: 1
    at: [54, 302]
    text: "{style=heading1}Transfer function"
  - id: t2
    page: 1
    at: [54, 328]
    text:
      width: full
      text: The output is taken across the capacitor. With the imaginary unit $j$, the gain in decibels and the phase in degrees follow from the modulus and the argument of $H$.
  - id: H
    page: 1
    at: [54, 360]
    math: "H(f) := 1/(1 + 2·π·f·τ·1j)"
  - id: G
    page: 1
    at: [240, 360]
    math: "G(f) := 20·log(|H(f)|)"
  - id: phi
    page: 1
    at: [410, 360]
    math: "φ(f) := arg(H(f))/deg@unit"
  - id: Gc
    page: 1
    at: [54, 398]
    math: "G(f_c) = "
  - id: phic
    page: 1
    at: [210, 398]
    math: "φ(f_c) = "
  - id: t3
    page: 1
    at: [54, 428]
    text:
      width: full
      text: At the cut-off the gain is $−3$ dB and the phase $−45°$. Above it the gain falls by $20$ dB for every decade of frequency.
  - id: k
    page: 1
    at: [54, 462]
    math: "k := 0, 0.01..4"
  - id: f
    page: 1
    at: [170, 460]
    math: "f_k(k) := 10^(1 + k)·Hz@unit"
  - id: pG
    page: 1
    at: [54, 490]
    plot:
      version: 1
      type: xy
      size: [238, 170]
      axes:
        x: {scale: log10, unit: "Hz", title: "Frequency"}
        y: {title: "Gain in dB"}
      traces:
        - {id: g, x: "f_k(k)", y: "G(f_k(k))", show_in_legend: false, line: {color: "#00428D"}}
      reference_lines:
        - {id: fc, axis: "x", value: 1591.55, line: {style: dash, color: "#8A97A6"}}
        - {id: g3, axis: "y", value: -3, line: {style: dash, color: "#8A97A6"}}
  - id: pP
    page: 1
    at: [303, 490]
    plot:
      version: 1
      type: xy
      size: [238, 170]
      axes:
        x: {scale: log10, unit: "Hz", title: "Frequency"}
        y: {title: "Phase in degrees"}
      traces:
        - {id: p, x: "f_k(k)", y: "φ(f_k(k))", show_in_legend: false, line: {color: "#01B5AF"}}
      reference_lines:
        - {id: fc, axis: "x", value: 1591.55, line: {style: dash, color: "#8A97A6"}}
        - {id: p45, axis: "y", value: -45, line: {style: dash, color: "#8A97A6"}}
